.. rubric:: Port and outputs

In a supported 3-D model this source automatically writes
``/ports/<id>`` (default ID ``portN``); no extra ``Rx`` is needed. A CPU or CUDA HSG
port is below ``/subgrids/<subgrid ID>/ports/<id>``. A hard source on a
domain-minimum transverse boundary remains an excitation but has no automatic
port because its full magnetic-current loop is unavailable. Reduced 2-D
sources do not provide this port output.

``Vtotal`` is gap voltage in volts, with sign opposite to the electric-field
component along the source edge. Use its stored ``time`` axis. Finite-resistance
ports use half-step-aligned voltage histories; hard ports retain voltage at
time zero and a separately staggered ``Iloop`` history on ``time_current``.
``frequency``, ``S11``, ``Zin`` and ``Yin`` are the corrected terminal spectra,
with separate ``valid_S11``, ``valid_Zin`` and ``valid_Yin`` masks.

The Python source's ``result`` provides ``time``, ``total_voltage``,
``frequency``, ``s11``, ``zin``, ``yin`` and their lowercase validity masks
after solving. The finite source's resistance is its reference impedance;
a hard source's independent reference impedance defaults to 50 Ohms.
A zero-amplitude finite-resistance source remains a passive load and reports
received voltage, but its own source-normalised S11/impedance is not a valid
input characterisation. Zero amplitude with zero resistance is a clamp.

.. code-block:: console

    python -m gprMax.toolboxes.Plotting.plot_port model.h5 --port feed --validity --save

Replace ``feed`` with the actual ID. See :ref:`voltage-port-theory` for
correction, timing and support details, :ref:`voltage-port-output` for the
schema, and :ref:`study-port` for a complete multiport matrix.
